    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Every treadmill moves a little under a runner; a long frame carrying a moving belt and a
          person landing on it a hundred and fifty times a minute is never going to be perfectly
          still. The question is whether yours moves more than it should, or in a way it did not
          used to. Usually the answer is in the four corners and the bolts, and the fix takes a
          spanner and a spirit level.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Why Is My Treadmill Shaking?</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill that shakes or wobbles is most often sitting unevenly: <strong>a levelling
            foot</strong> not quite touching the floor, <strong>an uneven floor or soft
            carpet</strong> letting one corner sink, or <strong>frame bolts</strong> that have
            settled since assembly. Those three account for most cases, and all are fixed from
            outside the machine. Less commonly the cause is <strong>loose upright or console
            bolts</strong>, <strong>worn deck isolators</strong>, a <strong>belt or roller
            problem</strong> such as a belt rubbing the frame or a bent roller, or an
            <strong>imbalance in the motor, flywheel or drive pulley</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The quickest way to tell outside from inside is to run the belt empty. If the machine
            is steady with nobody on it and shakes only when you walk or run, the cause is almost
            always support and structure — feet, floor, frame, deck mounts. If it vibrates with the
            belt running empty, and the vibration rises with speed, something that rotates is out
            of true.
          </p>
          <p>
            A thump or knock that keeps time with your steps or with the belt is a closely related
            problem with its own guide. If what you hear is more of an impact than a shake, our
            <a href="/treadmill-thumping-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">thumping and knocking guide</a>
            has the timing tests in detail. This page covers movement you can feel and see.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="name-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Shake, Wobble or Vibration? Name It First</h2>
<p>
            Owners use these words interchangeably, but they tend to describe three different
            things, and naming yours narrows the search quickly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Rocking.</strong> The whole machine tips slightly, usually diagonally, each
            time you land. You can often see one corner lift or the frame shift on the floor. This
            is support: feet, floor or carpet.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Wobble or sway.</strong> The base feels solid but the uprights, console or
            handrails move — side to side or front to back — as you run. Some of this is normal on
            lighter machines. A wobble that has increased, or one you can feel as play at a joint,
            is loose upright or console hardware.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Vibration or buzz.</strong> A fine, fast trembling you feel through your feet
            or the handrails, often with a hum, that changes with belt speed. If it is present with
            the belt running empty, it is rotating machinery: rollers, the drive pulley, the
            flywheel, the motor. If it is only present under load, it can be the deck or its
            mounts.
          </p>
          <p>
            Note when it started. Shake that has been there since the day the machine was built
            points at assembly and placement. Shake that appeared after a move, a fold, or someone
            working on the belt points at whatever was disturbed. Vibration that appeared on its own
            and is getting worse points at wear.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="levelling-feet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Levelling Feet and Uneven Floors</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill frame is long and sits on four or more points. Floors are rarely flat to
            the precision a long frame needs, and if one foot sits fractionally clear, the frame
            rocks onto it with each step. That is the single commonest cause of a shaking
            treadmill, and the cheapest to fix.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Find the short corner.</strong> With the machine unplugged, press down firmly on
            each corner of the frame in turn. A corner that moves, or a frame that rocks across a
            diagonal, has a foot not bearing weight.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Adjust the feet.</strong> Most treadmills have threaded levelling feet at the
            rear, and some have adjustable front feet or a levelling wheel. Wind the short corner's
            foot down until the frame sits solidly on all points and no longer rocks. Many manuals
            show the adjustment and, on some machines, a locknut that should be tightened once the
            foot is set.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check with a level.</strong> Put a spirit level across the frame at the front,
            at the rear, and along one side rail. Side to side should be level. Front to back need
            not be exactly level on every machine — some sit slightly higher at the front — but the
            frame should not be twisted.
          </p>
          <p>
            Levelling also helps a belt that keeps drifting to one side, because a twisted frame
            puts the rollers out of square and steers the belt. If you have been re-centring the
            belt every few sessions, level the machine before adjusting tracking again; our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to centre a treadmill belt</a>
            makes the same point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="carpet-floor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Carpet, Mats and Suspended Floors</h2>
<p>
            Levelling feet assume a firm floor. When the floor itself moves, adjustment alone will
            not hold.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Carpet.</strong> Carpet compresses under the feet at different rates depending
            on pile, underlay and where the weight sits. The heavy motor end sinks further, and a
            user who lands slightly to one side loads one rear corner more than the other. The
            frame ends up rocking on a surface that keeps changing. The usual answer is a dense,
            thin rubber equipment mat, and on deep pile or foam underlay a rigid board beneath the
            mat so the frame has a flat base. Our guide to a
            <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill on carpet</a>
            covers the thumb test for when a board is needed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The wrong mat.</strong> Soft interlocking foam tiles behave like carpet: they
            compress under the feet and add a second soft layer. A thick, spongy mat can make a
            treadmill feel less stable, not more. Our guide on
            <a href="/do-you-need-a-treadmill-mat/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether you need a treadmill mat</a>
            explains what to look for.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Suspended timber floors.</strong> Upstairs rooms and many older ground floors
            are timber on joists, and they flex under a runner. The whole floor can bounce, which
            feels like the treadmill shaking. Positioning the machine closer to a load-bearing wall
            or across the joists rather than in the middle of a span often helps; a firm mat
            reduces the transmitted vibration. If the shake is much worse at running pace than at a
            walk, and much louder in the room below, the floor is a large part of it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Against a wall or furniture.</strong> A treadmill touching a wall, radiator or
            piece of furniture will transmit vibration into it and rattle. Leave clearance on all
            sides — which you should anyway for safety at the rear.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="frame-bolts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Frame Bolts That Settled After Assembly</h2>
<p>
            New treadmills arrive partly assembled, and the owner typically fits the uprights,
            console and handrails. Those joints carry load with every stride, and bolts in new
            joints settle: paint compresses, washers bed in, and parts shift into their final
            positions. A machine that felt solid on day one can develop a wobble within the first
            few weeks for that reason alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Re-check after the first few sessions.</strong> With the machine unplugged, go
            round every bolt you fitted during assembly and every accessible frame fixing, and
            snug them. Many manuals recommend exactly this after initial use and then periodically;
            our <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance guide</a>
            puts it on the schedule.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Snug, not forced.</strong> Over-tightening strips threads, crushes tubing and
            cracks plastic shrouds. Tighten firmly with the tool supplied or a similar-sized
            spanner and stop there. Where the manual gives a torque figure, use it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Assembly order matters.</strong> If you are building a treadmill, the usual
            advice is to start every bolt in a joint by hand, leaving them loose until all are in,
            then tighten them evenly. Tightening one fully before the others are started can pull
            the parts slightly out of alignment and leave a joint under strain that never sits
            right. Our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-assemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to assemble a treadmill</a>
            covers the full sequence.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Missing hardware.</strong> If you find a washer or bolt on the floor, find where
            it came from. A missing fastener is a loose joint somewhere, and the shake is often
            that joint.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="uprights-console" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Uprights, Console and Handrail Wobble</h2>
<p>
            Some movement at the console is normal, and it is worth calibrating expectations before
            hunting for a fault. On lighter folding treadmills the uprights are relatively slender
            tubes cantilevered from the base, and a console that sways a little at running pace is a
            characteristic of the design rather than a defect. Heavier machines with thicker
            uprights and wider bases move much less. The difference is a large part of what you pay
            for between tiers, as our
            <a href="/cheap-vs-premium-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cheap versus premium comparison</a>
            explains.
          </p>
          <p>
            What is not normal is play: a console or handrail you can move by hand relative to the
            upright, an upright you can rock at its base joint, or a clunk as the console moves.
            That is loose hardware.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Upright-to-base bolts.</strong> These take the most load. They are usually at
            the bottom of each upright, sometimes behind a plastic cover. Snug them, both sides
            evenly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Console-to-upright fixings.</strong> The console usually bolts across the tops
            of the uprights, and the handrails may bolt to both. Check these with the console
            supported, and take care not to trap the console cable that runs down one upright —
            a pinched cable can cause a console fault later.
          </p>
          <p>
            One practical caution: leaning heavily on the handrails, or pulling on them while
            running, magnifies any looseness and can work joints loose faster. Rails are for
            balance, not for carrying your weight; our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-use-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to use a treadmill</a>
            covers why.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="folding-frames" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Folding Frames, Hinges and Latches</h2>
<p>
            Folding treadmills add a hinge between the deck and the base, a lift-assist mechanism,
            and a latch or locking pin. Each adds a possible source of movement.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The hinge pivot.</strong> The deck pivots on bolts or a shaft at the front. If
            those loosen, or the bushings wear, the deck can shift slightly with each step. This
            usually shows up as a knock or a sense that the deck moves independently of the base.
            Snug the pivot hardware as the manual describes; many pivots should not be fully
            clamped, because the deck must still fold, so follow the instructions rather than
            tightening hard.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The lock or latch.</strong> When unfolded, the deck rests in its running
            position, and on some machines it is locked there. Check that any latch or pin is fully
            engaged in the running position. A latch that is not fully home can clunk with every
            stride, and it is also a safety item.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The storage latch.</strong> Equally, when folded, the deck must be fully
            latched upright. A folded deck that is not secure can drop, which is a serious hazard
            in a house with children or pets. If the latch is damaged or does not engage
            positively, stop folding the machine until it is repaired.
          </p>
          <p>
            Folding machines that are moved frequently also tend to need their frame bolts checked
            more often. If yours lives folded against a wall and comes out daily, add a monthly
            check to the routine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-isolators" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Deck Isolators and Cushions</h2>
<p>
            Most home treadmills sit the deck on rubber or elastomer isolators between the deck and
            the frame. They absorb some of the footstrike and stop the deck transmitting every
            impact straight into the frame.
          </p>
          <p>
            When isolators harden, crack, compress permanently or fall out of position — all normal
            with age — the deck sits harder on the frame. The machine feels harsher and transmits
            more vibration into the floor, and a corner with a missing or flattened isolator can
            let the deck rock or bottom out under load. Where cushioning is adjustable, settings
            that differ from side to side can do the same.
          </p>
          <p>
            Inspection is the same as for a thumping deck: unplug, look along the underside of the
            deck from the side with a torch, and check each isolator is present and intact, and
            that the deck mounting bolts are snug but not over-tightened. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-thumping-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">thumping noise guide</a>
            covers the deck-mounting checks in full, and our explainer on
            <a href="/treadmill-cushioning-explained/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill cushioning</a>
            covers how the different systems work.
          </p>
          <p>
            Isolators are typically inexpensive parts for mainstream models. On a machine that has
            been flipped, had its deck replaced, or been worked on, assume the hardware went back
            differently and compare against the exploded diagram in the manual.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="belt-rollers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Belt Tracking, Rollers and Pulleys</h2>
<p>
            If the machine vibrates with the belt running empty, and the vibration rises with
            speed, something that rotates is not running true. Start with the belt, because it is
            the easiest to see.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A belt rubbing the frame.</strong> A belt that has tracked to one side can rub
            the side rail or the roller end cap, which produces a fine vibration and often a
            scraping sound. Look at both edges with the belt running empty at low speed. If it is
            off centre, correct it with the rear bolts: a quarter turn at a time, turning the bolt
            on the side the belt is moving toward clockwise. The full procedure is in
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to centre a treadmill belt</a>,
            and level the frame first if it keeps wandering.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A bent roller.</strong> A roller bent by a dropped weight, rough transport or
            the machine being tipped onto its end makes the belt rise and fall once per roller turn.
            You can often see it at the roller end as a wobble. It is a replacement part.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Worn bearings.</strong> A roller bearing with play lets the roller move under
            load and rumbles when empty. The commonest avoidable cause is a belt tensioned well
            past the two-to-three-inch lift test described in
            <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how tight a treadmill belt should be</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A loose front pulley.</strong> The front roller carries the pulley the motor's
            drive belt turns. If it works loose on its shaft, it wobbles once per turn. That is
            inside the motor hood and on most machines a service job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-flywheel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Motor, Flywheel and Drive Belt Imbalance</h2>
<p>
            Under the motor hood, the drive motor carries a flywheel and a pulley, and a short ribbed
            drive belt connects it to the front roller. Imbalance here produces a fast, rhythmic
            vibration or throb from the front of the machine that is present with the belt running
            empty and rises with speed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A loose or damaged flywheel.</strong> The flywheel smooths out the motor's
            delivery. If it has worked loose on the motor shaft, or picked up damage, it runs out of
            balance. This is the vibration on this page that should stop you using the machine: a
            flywheel loose on a spinning shaft is not something to run on.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor mounting.</strong> The motor sits on a bracket with bolts. If those
            loosen, the motor can move under changing load, which feels like a shudder each time
            you land or the speed changes. On many machines the motor's position also sets the
            drive-belt tension, so note where things sit before loosening anything.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Drive belt.</strong> A drive belt with a damaged section, or one that has
            stretched and slaps against the housing, produces a vibration at drive-belt frequency.
            A drive belt that slips usually announces itself as a jerk at start-up, which is
            covered in our guide to a
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-jerking/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill belt jerking or surging</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            All of this is inside the hood, which commonly voids a warranty when opened. With the
            machine unplugged and out of warranty, a visual check is reasonable; while it is in
            warranty, describe the symptom to support. If you see scorching, smell burning or the
            flywheel visibly moves on its shaft, stop there.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="from-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Vibration That Comes From Use, Not a Fault</h2>
<p>
            Some shake is the machine doing what it was built to do with the load it is given.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A light machine at running pace.</strong> Budget and compact treadmills are
            lighter, with narrower bases and slimmer uprights, and they move more under a runner.
            Walking pads and under-desk machines are designed for walking, and running on one
            produces movement no adjustment will cure. If you run regularly on a machine built for
            walking, the honest fix is a machine built for running; our guide to the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for running</a>
            covers what to look for.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Near the weight limit.</strong> A user close to the stated capacity loads the
            deck, frame and isolators harder, and a frame working near its limit flexes more. The
            capacity figure is a ceiling rather than a comfortable operating point, which our
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight capacity guide</a>
            explains.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A resonant speed.</strong> Some machines, on some floors, vibrate noticeably at
            one particular speed and settle above and below it. That is resonance between the
            stride, the frame and the floor. A firmer mat or a slightly different position in the
            room often moves it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Stride and foot placement.</strong> Heavy heel striking, running near the front
            of the deck, or an uneven gait load the frame unevenly. Running in the middle of the deck
            with a slightly quicker cadence usually reduces both noise and movement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-stop" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. When Vibration Means Stop</h2>
<p>
            Most shake is an annoyance and a maintenance job. A few kinds are signs that something
            could fail suddenly, and those are worth taking seriously.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Vibration that appeared suddenly and is severe, especially after a noise.</li>
            <li>A heavy, fast throb from the motor area with the belt running empty, or a flywheel
            you can see moving on its shaft.</li>
            <li>Vibration together with a burning smell, grinding or scraping from the front.</li>
            <li>A crack in the frame, a weld that has opened, or an upright that moves at its base
            joint after tightening.</li>
            <li>A folding latch or locking pin that does not engage fully.</li>
            <li>A belt with a visible bulge, tear or separating layers.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            In any of those cases, stop the machine, unplug it, and contact the manufacturer or a
            technician. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">troubleshooting guide</a>
            lists the four general stop-now symptoms — burning smells, tripping breakers, a belt
            that ignores the safety key and speed surging on its own — and a severe new vibration
            belongs in the same spirit.
          </p>
          <p>
            For everything else, the order is the one this page follows: corners, floor, bolts,
            uprights, hinge and latch, isolators, belt and rollers, and only then the motor. The
            first three resolve most cases without opening anything.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">13. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my treadmill shaking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most often it is sitting unevenly — a levelling foot not touching the floor, an uneven floor, or soft carpet compressing under one corner — or frame bolts have settled since assembly. Press each corner to find a short foot, adjust it until the frame sits solidly, check with a spirit level, then snug all the frame bolts with the machine unplugged.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my treadmill wobble when I run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some console and upright sway is normal on lighter folding machines. Play you can feel at a joint is not: snug the upright-to-base bolts and the console and handrail fixings. If the whole machine rocks, adjust the levelling feet. On carpet, a dense rubber mat and, on deep pile, a rigid board beneath it gives the frame a stable base.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my treadmill vibrating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Run the belt empty. If it vibrates with nobody on it and the vibration rises with speed, something rotating is out of true — a belt rubbing the frame, a bent roller, worn bearings, a loose pulley or flywheel. If it only vibrates under load, look at the feet, floor, frame bolts and deck isolators.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it normal for a treadmill to shake?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A little movement under a runner is normal, especially on lighter and folding machines. Rocking at a corner, play at a joint, vibration that has increased, or anything that appeared suddenly is not. A severe new vibration, a throb from the motor area or a frame that moves at a joint means stop using it and get it checked.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop my treadmill from wobbling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Unplug it, find and adjust any short levelling foot, check the frame with a spirit level, and snug every frame, upright and console bolt without over-tightening. On carpet use a dense thin rubber mat, adding a rigid board on deep pile. Then check deck isolators and belt tracking if it still moves.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an uneven floor make a treadmill shake?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is one of the commonest causes. A long frame on four points rocks onto any foot that sits slightly clear of the floor. An uneven floor also twists the frame slightly, which steers the belt to one side. Adjusting the levelling feet fixes most cases; a flexing suspended floor or soft carpet may also need a firm mat or board.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With a Shaking Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most shake is fixed from outside the machine. These four are how a simple fix becomes a bigger one.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adjusting the belt before levelling the frame</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A frame that rocks or twists steers the belt sideways, so tracking adjustments never hold and the belt edges wear from repeated correction. Level the machine first with the feet and a spirit level, then adjust tracking a quarter turn at a time if the belt still drifts.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Over-tightening frame bolts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Snugging bolts after assembly is right; forcing them is not. Over-tightening strips threads, crushes tubing, cracks shrouds and can clamp a folding pivot so it binds. Tighten firmly, stop, and use the manual's torque figure where it gives one.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a soft foam mat under it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Interlocking foam tiles and spongy mats compress under the feet like carpet does, so the frame rocks on a moving base. A dense thin rubber equipment mat, with a rigid board beneath on deep pile, gives the treadmill something stable to sit on.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running on a severe new vibration</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A heavy throb from the motor area, a flywheel moving on its shaft, or a frame or latch that moves at a joint can all fail suddenly. Stop, unplug and get it checked rather than seeing whether it settles.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

