        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Start at the corners.</strong> Find and adjust a short levelling
            foot, check the frame with a level, deal with soft carpet using a dense mat and board,
            and snug every frame and upright bolt. That resolves most shaking treadmills.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If it vibrates with the belt running empty, look at <strong class="text-white">belt
            tracking, rollers and the motor end</strong> — and stop if the vibration is severe and
            new. For impacts rather than shake, see the
            <a href="/treadmill-thumping-noise/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">thumping noise guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
